## ABSTRACT
In view of the increasing concerns for environmental problems caused by the widespread use of antibiotics, there is an urgent demand to develop effective technologies for antibiotics removal from pharmaceutical wastewater. For this purpose, photocatalysis has been developed as an important advanced oxidation technology possessing remarkable prospective, and thus the semiconductor photocatalysts are currently attracting unprecedented research attention. Herein, we report the fabrication of composite materials based on nitrogen–doped carbons and cadmium sulfide semiconductors (CdS/NC–T) derived solely from cadmium metal–organic frameworks (Cd–MOF) through a facile in situ carbonization method, as well as their application as viable photocatalysts towards tetracycline degradation under visible irradiation. It was revealed that cadmium and sulfur species in Cd–MOF precursors assembled to form CdS nanoparticles (NPs), which were deposited simultaneously on nitrogen–doped carbon scaffolds to afford CdS/NC–T composites during the carbonization processes. PXRD and TEM studies on as–prepared CdS/NC–T materials indicated the formation of crystalline hexagonal (Wurtzite) CdS NPs on the carbonaceous supports. These CdS/NC–T composites were further studied for photocatalytic tetracycline degradation in aqueous solutions under visible irradiation, among which CdS/NC–500 exhibited the highest TC degradation efficiency of ca. $83\%$ within $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . It was found that the photocatalytic performance of CdS/NC–T depended largely on the carbonization temperature, which suggested great potentials of the proposed synthetic pathway in engineering of new visible–light–driven photocatalysts.
KEYWORDS: cadmium sulfide; metal–organic framework; photocatalysis; antibiotics degradation
## INTRODUCTION
Pharmaceutical antibiotics and relative derivatives are emerging pollutants with public concerns originated from medicinal treatments for human as well as from farming industry1,2. Since most pharmaceutical antibiotics are poorly metabolized and can be absorbed by both humans and animals, their exposures will cause serious impacts on the ecosystem, as well as to develop and spread antibiotic–resistant bacteria (ARB) and antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) 3. Moreover, antibiotic residues might result in acute and chronic toxicity, and microorganism antibiotic resistance4,5. In order to address these issues6, development of effective technologies for the removal of antibiotics from aqueous phases has become extremely pressing. In this context, photocatalysis has been developed as an important advanced oxidation technology possessing remarkable prospective7–9 ， which readily leads to the development of efficient photocatalysts for environmental remediation10–12, with the aid of sustainable solar energy utilization13–15.
Cadmium sulfate (CdS) semiconductors as visible–light–driven photocatalysts are of current research hotspots due to their suitable band gap energy (ca. $2.4~\mathrm{eV}$ ) that guarantees activation under visible irradiation16–18. Meanwhile, photocatalysis in aqueous phases with CdS is often implicated by photocorrosion related to the instability of photocatalysts and release of toxic $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions to the environment19,20. In this context, the recombination of photogerated carriers are known to be responsible for photocatalysts deactivation and the formation of composite photocatalysts seems to be one of the main viable solutions. Of special notice, carbon materials have been extensively studied as one of the contemporary research hotspots because of their enormous applications in a wide variety of fields such as electrode materials21,22, adsorbents23,24, and catalyst supports25–27. Therefore, the use of carbon materials has been popular for composite synthesis. The preparation of carbon materials can be achieved by means of various synthetic pathways28–30, among which the top–down carbonization of self–assembled metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is attracting particular attention31–35. What might be the very first example using MOFs as templates to prepare engineered porous carbon was reported by Liu and co–workers36, in which the carbon materials exhibited high specific surface area and excellent electrochemical property. Jiang et al. developed a highly porous carbonaceous material showing excellent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption and electrochemical property by using MOF precursor as the template37, in the presence of furfuryl alcohol. Lou and co–workers designed hollow nanofibers of nitrogen–doped carbons derived from ultrafine ZIF–8 nanoparticles for high–performance supercapacitors38. Wang and colleagues reported excellent photodegradation efficiency of tetracycline antibiotics using composite photocatalysts prepared from in situ growth of MIL–100(Fe) on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoarrays39. More importantly, morphology of MOFs–derived carbonaceous materials can be programmed via the fine control of precursors33,40, pyrolysis temperatures41,42, auxiliary substances37, post–treatment methods43,44 etc., so as to achieve nanocomposites with enhanced property.
At present, it remains highly desirable and challenging to prepare carbon–based nanocomposites for further improving the performance of supported nanomaterials. Meanwhile, the multicomponent MOFs are ideal platforms for in situ growth of semiconductors nanoparticles that can simultaneously deposit on various carbon–rich scaffolds. In this work, a cadmium metal–organic framework (Cd–MOF) was used to fabricate CdS/NC–T composites at various temperatures by a facile in situ carbonization method. The Cd–MOF precursors functioned not only as cadmium and sulfur sources but also as template scaffolds of nitrogen–doped carbon supports. The as–prepared CdS/NC–T composites exhibited enhanced visible–light–driven catalytic degradation of tetracycline antibiotics, owing to the unique structural features and the desirable chemical compositions of the Cd–MOF precursors. This might be the first example employing cadmium metal–organic frameworks solely to prepare well–defined nitrogen–doped–carbon supported CdS nanomaterials for visible–light–driven antibiotics degradation.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis of Cadmium Metal–Organic Framework (MOF). The cadmium metal–organic framework, denoted as Cd–MOF, was synthesized through a modified procedure as follows45: 4–aminoazobenzene–4’–sulfonic monosodium salt ( $300~\mathrm{mg}$ , 10 mmol) was added to $40~\mathrm{mL}$ water and heated to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford a clear solution; a portion of $40~\mathrm{\mL}$ methanol solution of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $152\ \mathrm{\mg}$ , 5.0 mmol) was added drop–wisely, during which the orange precipitate (Cd–MOF) was formed and filtered after $30~\mathrm{min}$ , washed with mixed water–methanol solution (1:1; v/v) for three times, and dried overnight at $50~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ . Yield: ca. $122{\mathrm{~mg}}$ $(37\%)$ . IR (KBr, $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) spectra for Cd–MOF: 1678w, 1606s, 1498m, 1427w, 1397w, 1211s, 1169s, 1121s, 1032vs, 1007s, 852s, 834s, 792m, 709s, 624s, 595w, 576s, $547\mathrm{m}$ .
Preparation of Nitrogen–Doped–Carbon Supported Cadmium Sulfide Composites. The nitrogen–doped–carbon supported CdS materials were prepared from the carbonization of pre–treated Cd–MOF samples ( $200~\mathrm{mg}$ each) in tubular furnace at 400, 500, 600, or $700~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , respectively, for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere, washed with water, and dried overnight at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford CdS/NC–T composites, where T was the carbonization temperature. The selection of calcination temperatures was assisted by TGA and PXRD measurements (Figure S1), through which TGA was used to determine the range of rational calcination temperatures and PXRD was recorded to monitor the formation of CdS. Since the sublimation of CdS might well occur at high temperatures, calcination temperatures were therefore set below $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Structural Characterization of Cd–MOF. Reaction of dihydrous cadmium acetate and 4–aminoazobenzene–4’–sulfonic monosodium salt in 1:2 ratio afforded orange crystalline materials of Cd–MOF. Single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ –ray diffraction was applied to determine the structure of Cd–MOF with unit cell $a=5.9899(2)\textrm{\AA}$ , $b=7.103(2)\AA$ , c = 15.529(5) Å, $\alpha=101.441(6)^{\circ}$ , $\beta=99.923(1)^{\circ},\gamma$ $\gamma=$ $95.931(9)^{\circ}$ , and space group of $P{-}1$ . Moreover, PXRD pattern of the Cd–MOF bulk material matched well with the simulated one obtained from single crystal data (Figure S2), which suggested an excellent phase purity of the MOF material. According to the crystal structure previously described in the literature45, the structure of Cd–MOF is constructed by cadmium ions and organic ligands (mole ratio 1:2) that are aligned into distinctive domains. Such distribution of the inorganic and organic constituents in confined regions allowed the minimum re−arrangement of atoms, and thus in situ formation of well–defined nitrogen–doped–carbon supported CdS nanomaterials was successfully achieved through high temperature carbonization (Scheme 1).
Physical Characterization of CdS/NC–T Composites. Surface morphology of CdS/NC–T was determined by means of the SEM and TEM techniques. As shown in Figure 1a, CdS/NC–T composites retained the hollow rod–shaped morphology of Cd–MOF precursors, with CdS nanoparticles loaded on the surfaces (NPs, Figure 1b and 1c). A typical HRTEM image of CdS/NC–500, as shown in Figure 1d, identified a crystal spacing of $0.316~\mathrm{nm}$ , which was assigned to the (1 0 1) crystal plane of hexagonal CdS (Wurtzite). The particle size of CdS NPs was in the range of 6.0 to $13\ \mathrm{nm}$ (ca. $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ in average) which generally increased at a higher carbonization temperature (Figure S3, S4, and S5), however, an exorbitant temperature (i.e. over $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ might lead to the sublimation of CdS NPs and collapse of carbonaceous scaffolds. The crystal phases of CdS NPs were confirmed by the excellent match between experimental and simulated PXRD patterns. PXRD characteristics at $24.96^{\circ}$ , $26.64^{\circ}$ , $28.32^{\circ}$ , 36.74°, 43.82°, 48.04°, 51.02°, $52^{\circ}$ , $52.96^{\circ}$ , $54.76^{\circ}$ , and $58.62^{\circ}$ , which corresponded to lattice planes of (100), (002), (101), (102), (110), (103), (200), (112), (201), (004), and (202) of hexagonal CdS, respectively, were fully observed (Figure 2a). The distribution of elements in CdS/NC–T was studied by elemental mapping that also indicated uniform formation of CdS nanomaterials on nitrogen–doped–carbon supports (Figure S6). CdS loading of CdS/NC–T composites were determined by ICP results (Figure 2c) to be in the range of ca. $29.7\%$ (CdS/NC–500) to ca. $35.1\%$ (CdS/NC–600), which was in consistent with some previous studies $^{46,47}$ . The CdS contents in CdS/NC–T were fairly similar, although the composites were fabricated under significantly different carbonation temperatures48. It was noticeable that the C/N ratio in CdS/NC–T decreased from 4.2 to 8.1 with the carbonization temperature increment. Furthermore, $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms (77 K) revealed typical adsorption behaviors of mesoporous structures (Figure 2b) and the BET surface area of CdS/NC–T composites was determined as 84 to $135~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 2c), showing a rational decline with the increase of carbonization temperatures, possibly as a results of partial scaffold collapses of the carbonaceous supports at higher carbonization temperatures.
The XPS technique was applied to reveal surface compositions and valence state of elements in CdS/NC–T (Figure 3a, S7, S8, and S9). Taking the XPS spectra of CdS/NC–500 as a representative, the peak at $284.6\mathrm{eV}$ was ascribed to $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ carbon species (i.e., $\mathrm{Csp^{2}\mathrm{-}C s p^{2}}\rangle$ , and characteristics at 285.7,
287, and 289.1 eV were assigned to oxygenated and nitrogenated carbon species i.e. $\mathrm{Csp}^{3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{-}}$ 。 $\mathrm{Csp}^{3}\mathrm{=}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{Csp}^{2}{-}\mathrm{N}$ , and $\mathrm{Csp}^{3}{-}\mathrm{N}$ (Figure 3a)46,48. Two major characteristics were observed for N 1s at 398.5 and $400.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which corresponded to the pyridinic−N and pyrrolic−N, respectively (Figure 3b)48. The doublets at ca. 405.2 (Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\cdot$ ) and ca. 411.9 eV (Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ ) were the characteristics of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ (Figure $3\mathrm{c})^{46^{-48}}$ . Peaks at 162.8 $(\mathsf{S}~2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and $161.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ $\mathrm{(S~2p_{3/2})}$ in Figure 3d were from the spin−orbit doublets of sulfide $(\mathrm{S}^{2-})^{46-48}$ . These results further indicated that CdS/NC–T composites were successfully fabricated from the in situ carbonization of Cd–MOF precursors.
Photocatalytic Degradation of Tetracycline Antibiotics over CdS/NC–T. The photocatalytic activity of CdS/NC–T composites was studied towards tetracycline (TC) degradation under visible irradiation. Photocatalysts were first immersed in tetracycline solutions and reacted in dark for 30 min to reach the equilibrium of adsorption/desorption (Figure S10). The UV–vis absorption characteristics of tetracycline decreased gradually with time (Figure S11), in the presence of CdS/NC–T photocatalysts, indicating continuous photocatalytic TC degradation. It should be noticed that CdS/NC–T composites displayed significantly differentiable photocatalytic ability towards TC degradation. Of note, the CdS/NC–400 was able to degrade only ca. $20.5\%$ of antibiotics; CdS/NC–600 and CdS/NC–700 exhibited similar degradation efficiencies of approximately $72.6\%$ and $74.5\%$ , respectively; while the CdS/NC–500 achieved ca. $81.6\%$ of TC degradation within 1 h (Figure 4a). The carbonization temperature might be a determinant responsible for the distinguishable photocatalytic ability of CdS/NC–T, however, a moderate carbonization temperature of $500~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ clearly suggested the best performance photocatalyst for TC degradation. Quantitatively, the highest apparent rate constant $\kappa$ of $0.0639\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for CdS/NC–500 was calculated by the pseudo–first–order reaction kinetics equation (Figure 4b and Table S1), which was approximately 16 times of that for CdS/NC–400 $(0.0039\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ ). Moreover, the rate constant of CdS/NC–500 was significantly larger (5.3 times) than the $\mathrm{WO}_{3}/\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ Z–scheme photocatalysts showing similar TC degradation efficiencies49, yet comparable to the silver ions and carbon dots co–doped ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{WO}}_{6}$ ternary composite photocatalysts50. The excellent photocatalytic capacity of CdS/NC–500 was likely derived from the in situ growth of CdS NPs on the carbonaceous supports that facilitated interface photoadsorption and photocatalytic reactions. Meanwhile, enhanced separation efficiency of photogenerated charge carriers at host–guest interfaces might also be responsible for the optimized performance of photocatalysts.
Cyclic experiments of TC degradation in the presence of CdS/NC–500 was carried out to study the stability and reusability of the photocatalyst. It was clear that the photocatalyst retained over $80\%$ of its initial catalytic capacity after four consecutive runs of recycling reactions (Figure 4c). The slight loss of photocatalysts during recovery–and–reuse processes might account for the decrease of photocatalytic ability. PXRD patterns were recorded on the CdS/NC–500 photocatalyst before and after the recycling reactions, which matched well with each other and suggested considerable photocatalyst stability (Figure 4d).
Mechanism for Tetracycline Degradation over CdS/NC–500. UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) were first measured to study the photoabsorption behavior of as–prepared CdS/NC–T photocatalysts (Figure S12). The photocatalysts exhibited absorption edges appearing at ca. 662, 635, 614, and $678~\mathrm{nm}$ , calculated by the Tauc equation, which corresponded to band gap energy of ca. 1.87, 1.95, 2.02, and $1.83\ \mathrm{\eV}$ , respectively, which were fairly low by contrast to CdS–based photocatalysts and suggested promising photocatalytic capacity of these materials. Mott–Schottky plots for the best–performing photocatalyst CdS/NC–500 were recorded at 500, 1000 and $1500~\mathrm{Hz}$ , and the positive slope of $C^{-2}$ versus potential was in consistent with that of typical $n$ –type semiconductors (Figure 5a)51. The intersection point was independent on frequencies and the flat band potential $\mathrm{(V_{fb})}$ of CdS/NC–500 was estimated to be ca. –0.62 V vs. Ag/AgCl (–0.42 V vs. $\mathrm{NHE})^{52,53}$ , and the CB of CdS/NC–500 was thus approximately ca. –0.42V vs. NHE. Since the CdS/NC–500 possessed a band gap energy $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of ca. $1.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 5a, inset), the valence band (VB) was determined to be 1.53 V vs. $\mathrm{NHE^{54}}$ . The transient photocurrent plots (I–t) of CdS/NC–T were recorded, by which reversible current generation was recorded upon irradiation on/off (Figure 5b). The photocurrent decreased quickly to the initial levels when light was switched off. These results suggest that the electron–hole pairs in CdS/NC–T were efficiently separated, coupled with longer lifetime of photogenerated carriers due to the introduction of carbonaceous supports. It should also be noted that the photocurrent intensity was significantly increased for CdS/NC–T composites prepared at higher carbonization temperatures, suggesting better graphitization and conductivity of the carbonaceous supports.
The separation of photogenerated carriers are typically recognized as initial steps of photocatalytic reactions. Therefore, electrochemical analyses were carried out to investigate the effective transportation of photogenerated electrons and holes. Photoluminescence (PL) spectra of CdS/NC–T composites were recorded with excitation at $330~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S13a), among which the CdS/NC–500 possessed significantly decreased PL spectrum. These results indicated a more efficient transfer of photogenerated electrons and holes in CdS/NC–500. For further demonstrating the separation efficiency of photogenerated carriers, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) of CdS/NC–T composites were measured, as plotted in Figure 5c. As shown in the EIS Nyquist plot, the CdS/NC–500 displayed the smallest arc radius suggesting a low interfacial layer resistance, which was in good accordance with the PL results described above.
To demonstrate the catalytically active species in this photocatalytic system, we attempted to detect $0_{2}\bullet^{-}$ radicals by means of the ESR spin–trapping technique. Upon visible irradiation, the CdS/NC–500 produced significant characteristics of DMPO− $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ species, which supported the hypothesis of dominant $\mathrm{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ radicals (Figure 5d). Moreover, the presence of •OH was demonstrated by the fluorescence technique using terephthalic acid (TA) as a probe regent, which could react with •OH to generate detectable fluorescent 2–hydroxyterephthalicacid (TAOH; excitation: $315\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; emission: $425\mathrm{nm}$ ) upon time increase (Figure S13b)55,56. In such a way, the presence of •OH radicals during photocatalytic reactions was demonstrated.
For further investigation on the mechanism of the visible–light–driven tetracycline degradation, various scavengers were used to capture catalytically active species being generated during the photocatalytic reactions57,58. As shown in Figure 5e, notable decreases of photocatalytic efficiency were observed with scavengers i.e. ammonium oxalate (AO; for $h^{+}$ ), isopropyl alcohol (IPA; for •OH) and 1,4–benzoquione (BQ; for $\mathrm{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ )59. The AO exhibited a significant quenching effect towards TC degradation, which was noticeably significant than IPA and BQ. The contribution of various scavengers to overall photocatalytic efficiency was estimated to be ca. $54\%$ $(h^{+})$ , ca. $32\%$ (•OH) and ca. $20\%$ $(\mathbf{O}_{2}\bullet^{-})$ . These results indicated $h^{+}$ was likely more dominant radical species than •OH and $0_{2}\bullet^{-}$ .
In order to reveal the degradation pathway of tetracycline, the HPLC–MS technique was applied for quantitative analyses of the intermediate species in photocatalytic processes60. A number of possible intermediates (B to F) were identified by the mass spectra during photocatalytic TC degradation (Table S2). The formation of these intermediates was rationalized as follows: TC $[\mathbf{m}/z=$
445) molecules were first oxidized by dominant radicals, such as $h^{+}$ , •OH and $\mathrm{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ , to trigger ring opening reaction that generated species B $\mathbf{\tilde{m}}/z=417$ ); secondary decomposition species C $({\bf{m}}/z=$ 265) and D ${\bf\langle m/z=}223.$ ) were subsequently produced from the detachment of carbonyl and amino (C) groups in B and further oxidation $(\mathrm{D})^{61}$ ; an alternative degradation pathway might exist since species E $(\mathrm{m}/z\:=\:367)$ ) and $\mathrm{~F~}$ $(\mathrm{m}/z=351\$ ) were observed, which were likely the oxidative species of intermediate B and converted to carbonyl products; finally, small molecular weight species were completely decomposed to form end products $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
Based on the above experimental results, The photocatalytic TC degradation pathway is proposed as follows: electrons first transfer from the VB to CB of CdS upon visible irradiation, and the photogenerated $e^{-}$ and $h^{+}$ take part in the generation of catalytically active species such as, •OH, and $\mathrm{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ , by which tetracycline antibiotics are oxidized and decomposed (Figure 5f)62.
