On the other hand, the carbonaceous supports might effectively promote the accumulation and access of antibiotics on the interfaces of CdS NPs and carbonaceous supports. In such a way, the surface–adsorbed antibiotics can be degraded by accepting active species from the photocatalysts under visible irradiation.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this work, novel nitrogen–doped–carbon supported cadmium sulfide (CdS/NC–T) composites have been successfully prepared via a facile in situ carbonization method by using cadmium metal–organic frameworks as precursors. These composites are successfully applied as viable photocatalysts for tetracycline (TC) degradation under visible irradiation. The photocatalytic degradation efficiency of TC over CdS/NC–500 has been determined to be ca. $83\%$ in $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ with an apparent rate constant $\kappa$ of $0.0639\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , which is comparable to some of the best–performing composite photocatalysts. The improved photocatalytic performance of CdS/NC–500 is tentatively ascribed to the efficient production and transportation of photogenerated carriers ( $\textcircled{h^{+}}$ and $e^{-}$ ) due to the enhanced interface interactions derived from in situ growth of CdS nanoparticles on the carbonaceous supports. Cyclic experiments indicate the noticeable reusability and stability of the material photocatalysts for TC degradation. We believe highly efficient semiconductor/NC photocatalysts can be expected, based on the proposed synthetic pathway, for applications in environmental remediation and related fields, since the structure and morphology of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are well–known to be readily tuned by judicious selection of metal ions and rational design of organic constituents. Further study focusing on the effect of Cd–MOF precursors to photocatalytic performance of composite derivatives is currently underway.
